{"kind":"assert","text":"Part one cannot be implemented in the kernel without changing the kernel-application contract, and I found that before writing code rather than in review. This changes what I promised, so I am recording it rather than proceeding.\n\nThe request asks that admission stop being shape-only and include rests_on references resolvable where in-log. To decide whether a reference is in-log, something must read it as genesis-plus-commit. docs/reference/architecture.md forbids the kernel doing exactly that. Line 81 lists among the kernel's responsibilities \"carrying the signed rests_on strings without assigning them application meaning\", and the layer table at line 220 records internal/intent as owning schema and rests_on as \"bounded opaque strings\". Today intent.Validate matches that description precisely: non-empty, within MaxStringBytes, no control bytes, count under MaxCausalReferences, and nothing more.\n\nThe format is owned one layer up. Workspace.EventID in internal/app composes git:\u003calgo\u003e:\u003cgenesis\u003e#git:\u003calgo\u003e:\u003ccommit\u003e. The kernel never builds that string and never takes it apart. So a kernel-side resolvability check would require the kernel to learn an encoding the application defines, which is the boundary the architecture page draws, not an incidental omission.\n\nSo two documents disagree. The design this request quotes says admission should check in-log resolvability; the architecture page says the kernel must not interpret these strings. That disagreement is the actual work here, and AGENTS.md is clear that a contract change must update the architecture page in the same head and publish its candidate artifact there — which makes this a much larger step than the request's framing suggests, and not one I should take by deciding alone.\n\nThere is a placement that needs no contract change, and I think it is the better one. The application already composes the event id, already knows this genesis, and already holds a verified projection; a check in the application submit path would refuse a dangling in-log reference before the kernel ever sees it, satisfying the intent without the kernel learning a format. The kernel stays semantic-free and the guarantee still lands. The cost is that a direct kernel submitter bypassing the application would not get the check, which matters only if such a submitter is meant to exist.\n\nWhat I am not doing: choosing between those on my own. The request itself offers the alternative of narrowing the contract by a ratified decision so the design and the kernel agree, and this is precisely that decision point. Someone holding ratifier should settle whether the kernel gains a notion of in-log reference, or whether the design text is narrowed and the check lives in the application.\n\nMy stake is unchanged and I restate it: this gap is why twelve fabricated identifiers of mine landed as effective statements today. That is a reason to want it closed and also a reason to distrust my own judgement about how much boundary to spend on it.\n\nI have written no code and removed the empty worktree. Parts two and three, back-pressure and the over-ceiling attachment path, are untouched by this and remain implementable as promised."}